course: dictionary
lesson: 5983
title: Maze
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A maze is a puzzle made of paths or passages with walls or hedges. You have to find the way through without getting lost. «Fish can learn simple mazes and remember the way, sometimes using landmarks like stones or plants.» A landmark is something you can see that helps you find your way.
explain: Maze can mean more than one thing. Sometimes it is a real place, like a hedge maze in a park. Sometimes it is a puzzle on paper, where you trace a line from the start to the finish. It can also mean any confusing set of things, like «a maze of rules».
explain: To choose the right meaning, read the words around the word. Then try your meaning in the sentence. If "a confusing set of things" fits, you have found the meaning.
fact: A maze is a puzzle made of paths or passages with walls or hedges.
fact: «Dogs, cats, rats and birds have this busy stage too, and scientists have even watched sleeping rats replay a maze in their brains.»
fact: «The three-second memory is a myth: goldfish have been trained to push levers, swim through small mazes and come to a sound at feeding time, and they remember for months.»
fact: In a Greek myth, the hero Theseus used a ball of thread to find his way out of a maze called the labyrinth.
fact: Maze can also mean any confusing set of things, such as a maze of streets or a maze of rules.
exercise: What is a maze? | A loud noise at feeding time | *A puzzle of paths with walls or hedges, where you must find the way through | A kind of fish | A ball of thread
exercise: «A maze of rules» means rules that are | *So confusing that it is hard to find your way | Made of leaves and hedges | Drawn as a line from start to finish | Easy to remember for months
exercise: «Fish can learn simple mazes and remember the way, sometimes using landmarks like stones or plants.» What is a landmark? | *Something you can see that helps you find your way | A loud sound | A paper trail | A type of fish
test: In the myth, «Theseus used a ball of thread to find his way out of a maze». What did the thread help him do? | Catch a fish | Build a wall | Draw a picture | *Find his way out of the maze
test: On a paper maze, what do you do to solve it? | Swim through the paths | *Trace a line from the start to the finish | Push a lever | Count the hedges
exam: A teacher says, «The homework was a maze of questions.» What does maze mean here? | A paper puzzle with a line to trace | *A confusing set of things | A kind of fish | A hedge in a garden
